<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Ceylon</h1><p class="st-meta">1640 CE – 1796 CE · capital Colombo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dutch East India Company's colony on the coasts of Sri Lanka, run for its cinnamon monopoly and leaving a lasting legal and architectural mark.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasinha-ii-of-kandy">Rajasinha II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kandy">Kingdom of Kandy</a> invited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>) to help expel the Portuguese. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batticaloa">Batticaloa</a> in 1638, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a> in 1639 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> in 1640, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> in 1656 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> in 1658, but kept the conquered coasts for themselves rather than handing them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> ruled the maritime provinces, enforcing a strict monopoly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>, the colony's chief product, and also trading elephants, pearls and areca. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-dutch-law">Roman-Dutch law</a>, introduced in this period, still forms the basis of Sri Lankan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> built forts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a>, Reformed churches and canals, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">Burgher</a> community of European descent emerged. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> alternated between trade and war.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>, British forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> possessions in 1795 to 1796. They were formally ceded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a> in 1802 and became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-ceylon">British Ceylon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle-fort">Galle Fort</a> is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-ceylon.jpg" alt="Dutch Ceylon" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1640 CE – 1796 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Colombo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rijcklof van Goens, Gustaaf Willem van Imhoff</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Church, Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Sinhala, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Sri Lanka</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dutch Sri Lanka, VOC Ceylon</dd></dl></aside></div>
